WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.180
Imagine preparing many identical spin one-half
systems.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.760
Measuring along the vertical axis gives sixty
percent plus outcomes.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.280
Have you completely identified the quantum
state?

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:10.940
Surprisingly, no.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.400
To understand why, we need the formalism:
states, operators, probabilities,

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.820
measurement updates, and time evolution.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.980
Our small two-level laboratory will turn those
postulates into predictions you can

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:24.260
test.

00:00:25.353 --> 00:00:29.633
A pure state is represented by a normalized
ket in a complex Hilbert space.

00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:35.253
A bra is its conjugate transpose, so the inner
product includes complex conjugation.

00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:40.233
In an orthonormal two-state basis, the
coefficients are probability amplitudes.

00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:42.593
Their squared magnitudes sum to one.

00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:46.493
Changing basis changes the coordinates, not
the preparation.

00:00:47.193 --> 00:00:51.033
A common overall phase also leaves every
measurement probability unchanged.

00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:53.313
Pause and predict.

00:00:54.213 --> 00:00:56.433
Spin can be measured along three spatial axes.

00:00:57.093 --> 00:00:59.273
Does that make its spin Hilbert space
three-dimensional?

00:00:59.973 --> 00:01:04.373
Choose between two dimensions, three
dimensions, and infinitely many dimensions.

00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:08.843
Distinguish the number of possible apparatus
orientations from the number of

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:12.043
independent states needed to describe this
degree of freedom.

00:01:19.053 --> 00:01:21.493
For spin one-half alone, the answer is two.

00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:25.493
The plus and minus vertical-spin states form a
complete basis.

00:01:26.253 --> 00:01:29.853
Measurements along other directions use
different bases in that same space.

00:01:30.673 --> 00:01:34.153
Position and other degrees of freedom require
additional structure; we have

00:01:34.153 --> 00:01:35.453
deliberately excluded them.

00:01:35.893 --> 00:01:38.613
The Bloch arrow summarizes a two-level state.

00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:42.393
It is not a tiny spinning ball or a particle
trajectory.

00:01:43.587 --> 00:01:47.267
An observable is represented here by a
Hermitian operator.

00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:51.767
Its eigenvalues are possible results of an
ideal projective measurement.

00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:57.867
Its eigenvectors, or more generally its
eigenspaces, describe states with definite

00:01:57.867 --> 00:01:58.427
values.

00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:01.267
Hermiticity gives real eigenvalues.

00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:06.597
Completeness of the orthogonal spectral
projectors guarantees that the probabilities

00:02:06.597 --> 00:02:09.437
for all mutually exclusive outcomes add to
one.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.720
The Pauli z matrix has eigenvalues plus one
and minus one.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.300
Physical z spin is h-bar divided by two times
that matrix, so the measured angular

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.940
momenta are plus or minus h-bar over two.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.340
H-bar is Planck's constant divided by two pi.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:32.230
Never silently exchange a dimensionless Pauli
expectation with a physical spin

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:32.650
value.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:37.173
Prepare the horizontal plus state and measure
vertical spin once.

00:02:37.853 --> 00:02:41.213
Can the detector report zero because the
average vertical spin is zero?

00:02:41.873 --> 00:02:46.893
Predict: zero every time, either plus or minus
h-bar over two, or any value between

00:02:46.893 --> 00:02:47.513
those extremes.

00:02:48.163 --> 00:02:52.123
Give a reason based on the observable, not on
a classical picture of an arrow.

00:02:59.153 --> 00:03:04.053
The detector reports one of the two
eigenvalues, each with probability one half in
this

00:03:04.053 --> 00:03:04.613
preparation.

00:03:05.613 --> 00:03:09.173
Zero is the expectation value, not a possible
single-shot result.

00:03:09.743 --> 00:03:14.363
Writing an operator in its spectral form, as
eigenvalue times projector summed over

00:03:14.363 --> 00:03:16.983
outcomes, makes this distinction explicit.

00:03:17.903 --> 00:03:22.883
Degenerate eigenvalues correspond to whole
eigenspaces rather than a uniquely selected

00:03:22.883 --> 00:03:23.583
eigenvector.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.080
For an ideal measurement, the probability of
an outcome is the squared length of the

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.880
state projected into its eigenspace.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.880
For a nondegenerate outcome, this is the
squared magnitude of the inner product with

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:36.500
its eigenket.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.140
Amplitudes can be complex or negative.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:40.660
Probabilities cannot.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:45.850
Taking a coefficient directly as a probability
can give an impossible answer.

00:03:46.953 --> 00:03:51.513
Our example has vertical plus probability zero
point six and vertical minus probability

00:03:51.513 --> 00:03:52.613
zero point four.

00:03:53.173 --> 00:03:57.213
Its relative phase does not appear in those
two numbers, but it matters in other bases.

00:03:57.873 --> 00:04:02.253
If an outcome is recorded, the conditional
post-measurement state is the projected ket

00:04:02.253 --> 00:04:03.353
divided by its norm.

00:04:04.053 --> 00:04:08.273
An impossible outcome has no conditional
state; division by zero is not physics.

00:04:09.553 --> 00:04:12.093
Suppose the first measurement records
horizontal plus.

00:04:12.093 --> 00:04:16.013
Immediately measure vertical spin, with no
intervening evolution.

00:04:16.813 --> 00:04:21.233
Is the probability of vertical plus still zero
point six, now one half, or now one?

00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:24.353
Predict using the state after the first
recorded result.

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:28.743
Reusing the original preparation is a common
but consequential mistake.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.420
The probability is one half.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.340
Conditional on horizontal plus, the state is
the horizontal plus eigenstate, regardless

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.740
of the original amplitudes.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.040
If we repeat the same horizontal measurement
immediately, its recorded value repeats

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.860
with certainty in this ideal model.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:57.250
If we discard an intermediate outcome instead,
we must average over its possible

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.610
post-measurement states, rather than
pretending nothing happened.

00:05:01.753 --> 00:05:05.933
An expectation value combines the possible
outcomes with their Born probabilities.

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:11.633
For our vertical measurement, sixty percent
plus h-bar over two and forty percent minus

00:05:11.633 --> 00:05:15.633
h-bar over two give an average of zero point
one h-bar.

00:05:16.233 --> 00:05:19.233
That average describes many identically
prepared systems.

00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:24.113
It does not mean that each system contains a
little fraction of each detector result.

00:05:25.253 --> 00:05:28.513
Compute the expectation as bra, operator, ket.

00:05:29.153 --> 00:05:32.673
Then independently compute the
probability-weighted eigenvalue sum.

00:05:33.153 --> 00:05:34.073
They must agree.

00:05:34.543 --> 00:05:38.923
For one thousand independent preparations, the
expected number of vertical plus

00:05:38.923 --> 00:05:43.483
outcomes is six hundred, with a binomial
standard deviation of about fifteen point

00:05:43.483 --> 00:05:44.123
five.

00:05:44.683 --> 00:05:49.683
A simulated histogram illustrates sampling
fluctuations; it is not a claim of measured

00:05:49.683 --> 00:05:50.563
laboratory data.

00:05:51.587 --> 00:05:54.647
The predicted mean vertical spin is zero point
one h-bar.

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:55.967
Which statement follows?

00:05:56.667 --> 00:06:01.107
Every individual detector result equals that
mean, the mean is approached statistically

00:06:01.107 --> 00:06:06.147
across many preparations, or exactly six
hundred of every thousand results must be

00:06:06.147 --> 00:06:06.387
plus?

00:06:06.977 --> 00:06:11.197
Choose the statement that respects both the
operator spectrum and random sampling.

00:06:18.253 --> 00:06:19.713
The second statement is correct.

00:06:20.453 --> 00:06:25.473
The expectation is a probability-weighted
theoretical mean; a finite sample fluctuates.

00:06:26.353 --> 00:06:30.233
Increasing the number of independent trials
narrows the uncertainty of the estimated

00:06:30.233 --> 00:06:33.493
mean, but does not create new single-shot
eigenvalues.

00:06:34.203 --> 00:06:39.463
In your notebook, change the sample size and
seed, report uncertainty, and compare the

00:06:39.463 --> 00:06:41.723
observed deviation with the binomial
prediction.

00:06:42.987 --> 00:06:44.147
Prepare vertical plus.

00:06:44.887 --> 00:06:47.567
An immediate vertical measurement gives plus
with certainty.

00:06:48.247 --> 00:06:50.867
Now insert a horizontal measurement and ignore
its result.

00:06:51.527 --> 00:06:55.687
The resulting ensemble is an equal mixture of
horizontal plus and horizontal minus.

00:06:56.307 --> 00:06:59.127
A later vertical measurement gives plus only
half the time.

00:06:59.617 --> 00:07:03.497
Ignoring the record is not the same operation
as omitting the apparatus.

00:07:04.753 --> 00:07:10.273
For finite-dimensional Hermitian observables,
commuting operators can be simultaneously

00:07:10.273 --> 00:07:11.433
diagonalized.

00:07:12.113 --> 00:07:14.393
Horizontal and vertical spin do not commute.

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:17.213
Their ideal measurement order can therefore
matter.

00:07:17.733 --> 00:07:23.033
But a zero expectation of their commutator in
one specially chosen state does not make

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:24.653
the commutator operator zero.

00:07:25.993 --> 00:07:29.993
Compatibility concerns the operators, not just
one number calculated in one

00:07:29.993 --> 00:07:30.473
preparation.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.500
Return to vertical plus preparation.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.900
Insert the horizontal measurement, discard its
result, and then measure vertical spin.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.640
Is vertical plus certain, impossible, or
obtained with probability one half?

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.640
Use the complete experimental sequence,
including the disturbance associated with the

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:49.640
intermediate measurement.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.940
State which idealizations your prediction
assumes.

00:08:00.087 --> 00:08:01.387
The answer is one half.

00:08:02.007 --> 00:08:06.667
You can calculate each conditioned branch and
then average, or use the density matrix

00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:08.247
after the unrecorded measurement.

00:08:08.887 --> 00:08:10.347
Those independent routes agree.

00:08:11.027 --> 00:08:14.947
Our assumptions are ideal projective
measurements and no evolution between them.

00:08:15.387 --> 00:08:18.627
A real apparatus may have additional errors or
dynamics.

00:08:19.407 --> 00:08:22.987
Testing this model requires checking those
conditions rather than treating every

00:08:22.987 --> 00:08:24.707
discrepancy as a new postulate.

00:08:25.853 --> 00:08:30.753
For an isolated system, the Schrödinger
equation generates unitary time evolution.

00:08:31.593 --> 00:08:34.953
A time-independent Hamiltonian gives an
exponential operator.

00:08:35.593 --> 00:08:39.253
An energy eigenstate acquires only an overall
phase, leaving

00:08:39.253 --> 00:08:42.233
time-independent-observable probabilities
unchanged.

00:08:42.953 --> 00:08:47.413
A superposition of different energies
accumulates relative phase, which can change

00:08:47.413 --> 00:08:49.373
interference and expectation values.

00:08:50.113 --> 00:08:53.593
Stationary does not mean that the ket has
literally stopped acquiring phase.

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.880
Start in horizontal plus and choose the
Hamiltonian proportional to the Pauli z
matrix.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.420
Write the dimensionless rotation angle as
omega times time.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:10.400
Vertical probabilities stay one half, but
horizontal plus probability oscillates as

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.140
cosine squared of half that angle.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.540
At a pi rotation the state is horizontal
minus.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.840
Unchanged vertical statistics alone would miss
this entire change.

00:09:20.987 --> 00:09:24.527
After that pi rotation, what is the
probability of horizontal plus?

00:09:25.207 --> 00:09:27.227
Predict zero, one half, or one.

00:09:27.847 --> 00:09:30.687
Then ask why the vertical measurement still
gives equal probabilities.

00:09:31.167 --> 00:09:32.387
Keep track of relative phase.

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:37.307
A conclusion about every observable cannot be
inferred from measurements in a single

00:09:37.307 --> 00:09:37.647
basis.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.020
Horizontal plus probability is zero.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:52.620
In the laboratory, compare the analytic
rotation with eigenvalue exponentiation and

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.000
direct integration of the Schrödinger
equation.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.340
Check norm conservation and solver
convergence.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:04.310
Changing omega changes the time scale;
confusing radians, cycles and seconds changes

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:04.870
the prediction.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.910
Use the stated units and verify the limiting
case at time zero.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.140
We can now revisit the opening puzzle.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:16.520
Vertical probabilities determine populations,
but not the relative phase.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:21.340
Two different pure states can agree on those
data and disagree in another basis.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.820
Measuring horizontal and transverse spin
provides additional information.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:30.970
Inferring a state requires a model of
preparation, a sufficient set of measurements,

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:35.630
uncertainty estimates, and an honest statement
of what remains undetermined.

00:10:36.853 --> 00:10:42.153
Our laboratory represents a pure two-level
state, an ideal observable and a

00:10:42.153 --> 00:10:43.693
defined sequence of operations.

00:10:44.673 --> 00:10:49.993
Normalize the state, find spectral projectors,
compute probabilities, condition on

00:10:49.993 --> 00:10:52.773
recorded outcomes, and evolve between
measurements.

00:10:53.473 --> 00:10:58.713
Do not apply the pure-state picture blindly to
an unrecorded ensemble or a system

00:10:58.713 --> 00:11:00.073
entangled with its environment.

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:03.233
Those cases require a broader state
description.

00:11:04.453 --> 00:11:07.913
Two preparations give identical vertical-spin
probabilities.

00:11:08.613 --> 00:11:10.793
Must all their measurement predictions be
identical?

00:11:11.533 --> 00:11:16.473
Choose yes, no because relative phase may
differ, or yes because normalization fixes

00:11:16.473 --> 00:11:17.033
the phase.

00:11:17.693 --> 00:11:20.373
Propose an additional measurement that could
distinguish the preparations.

00:11:21.033 --> 00:11:25.693
A good answer states what the available
evidence does and does not determine.

00:11:32.787 --> 00:11:35.167
Relative phase can distinguish those
preparations.

00:11:36.047 --> 00:11:40.507
Open the browser Python lab, vary the state
and measurement axis, and test your

00:11:40.507 --> 00:11:41.987
predictions before running the code.

00:11:42.627 --> 00:11:46.147
Then tackle an unfamiliar three-level
measurement with a degenerate outcome.

00:11:46.677 --> 00:11:50.957
Decide which coherence an ideal measurement
preserves, compare independent

00:11:50.957 --> 00:11:53.337
calculations, and defend the assumptions.

00:11:54.097 --> 00:11:58.537
The postulates become useful when you can
explain a new experiment, not merely repeat

00:11:58.537 --> 00:11:59.037
their names.
